{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '365,620p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md; sed -n '130,158p' collab/phase8-plan.md; tail -5 /tmp/phase8d-slow.log; rg -n 'rows-support' test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e*\"","aggregated_output":"  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n| descriptor rows `:hash :sha256` at `debruijn_code.cljc:138`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:200`, `debruijn.cljc:139-140` (and the `:node-hash` prose string) | yes | yes: becomes `:hash :blake3`; this is a second descriptor row change beside `:dim/lift-to` (Q4) |\n| docstrings `debruijn_code.cljc:552-560`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:262`, `debruijn.cljc:1021` | | rewrite |\n| `dao/jing.cljc` `sha256`, `sha256-bytes`, registry `:sha256` | yes, explicitly mintable registry member (owner) | no |\n| `dao/jing/content/step.cljc:288` `{:algorithm :sha256}` option | yes | no |\n\nTests mentioning SHA-256 inside yin.vm (counts): `linker_test` 8, `engine_test` 4, `completion_test` 3, `debruijn_test` 2, `malformed_rows` 2, and 1 each in `vm_test`, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test` (dies), `debruijn_register_compile_test` (dies), `semantic_test` (dies), `ucf/checkpoint_test` (dies), `ucf/rows_checkpoint_test`, `linker/head_follow_test`. Each must be read: most mint a `sha256` address deliberately to test the registry seam and stay; those that compute H/R by hand move. `dao.*` tests (jing, btree, space) are out of scope.\n\nNo `jing/digest-string :sha256` call exists in yin.vm; the move is six `jing/sha256` to `jing/blake3` edits plus descriptor rows plus prose.\n\n### 1.7 Switch points\n\n| Switch | Today | Phase 8 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `linker/relowered` | `linker.cljc:2159-2177`: `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` then `linearize/adapt` / `register-compile/adapt` | `(derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project-rows tree)))`, `(derive/register-image …)`; `verified-policy-outcome` `:2233` `(= :yin.semantic/code format-kw)` keeps its branch |\n| `relowered-hashes`, `verify-same-root-pairing`, `fallback-fetch` | `linker.cljc:2457-2565` (old H/R request paths: `resolve/resolve` then `lower-stack`/`lower-register` from source datoms) | these are the \"old H/R request paths\" of §10 item 8: delete `relowered-hashes` and the `resolve` require; `verify-same-root-pairing` recomputes from A: `(derive/* (:vector (sr-linearize/project-datoms source-datoms)))`. Tests: `linker_test.cljc:1969-1980`, `linker_manifest_test.cljc:740-743` |\n| `checked-derivation` | `linker.cljc:2252-2262`: record input must be the tree | de Bruijn records: input is A (the semantic derivation's output); semantic record unchanged (§7.6) |\n| `implemented-profiles` | `linker.cljc:952-958`; profile maps `:930-948` keep `\"stack-lowering\"`/`\"register-lowering\"` (ruling 2) | docstrings only |\n| `semantic-format` / `semantic-register-format` | `linker.cljc:695-731`; `interpreter.cljc:84-93` holds 7 records; `serve_test.cljc:446-452` asserts 7 | one record `semantic-format` with `:format :yin.semantic/code :contract \"v4\"` and the v4 scanners; `interpreter/formats` holds 6; `serve_test` asserts 6 |\n| `publish-closure!` | `publish.cljc:115-213`: `linearize/lower-rows` for the semantic vector, `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` on named datoms for H/R, `derive-record tree-address output` for all three, index `{h h-addr r r-addr}` `:189` | `sem-vector` from `sr-linearize/project-rows tree`; `h-image`/`r-image` from `derive` over that vector; records `A -> H`, `A -> R` (`:yin.ledger/input` = `sem`); index `{:yin.debruijn.code h-addr :yin.debruijn.register r-addr}`; contracts `\"v4\"` |\n| `address-attribute`/`index-from-datoms` | `linker.cljc:965-990` | re-minted; keyed by format |\n| REPL kernel table | `repl.cljc:143-146` `vm-constructors` (`semantic/create-vm`, `stack/create-vm`, `register/create-vm`), `:224-240` `program-loaders` (`linearize/rows-loader semantic/load-vector`; `debruijn-linearize/adapt`, `register-compile/adapt` over `packet->named-datoms`) | `:semantic` to the new `create-vm` and `load-vector` over `(:vector (sr-linearize/project-rows (macro/packet->row-set packet)))`; `:stack`/`:register` to `derive/stack-image`/`register-image` over the same vector; `packet->named-datoms` `:150-156` deleted; closes the REPL custody gap (UCF §7.11 note at `universal-continuation-format.md:3347`) |\n| `ledger/lowering-profile` and the derivation check | `ledger.cljc:40-50` (`\"ast-to-bytecode\"`, contract `\"v3\"`), `:194-204` (`code/well-formed-vector?`, `linearize/lower-rows`) | contract `\"v4\"`; check uses `sr-code/well-formed?` and `sr-linearize/project-rows` |\n| the `raise` dependency | the phrase comes from the frozen design (§10 item 8, F14 insertion point at `derivations.md:1267`); no `raise` symbol exists in `semantic_register*.cljc` or `linker.cljc` today | I read it as the old kernel's thrown-`ex-info` error path that `linker/relowered` swallows (`:2177` `catch … nil`) and `relowered-hashes` swallows (`:2466`); it leaves with those functions. Noted so the implementer does not hunt for a function named `raise` |\n\n### 1.8 Build tooling, Dart and docs touched by the rename\n\n- `src/dev/subsystems.edn` matches by prefix `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.ucf`; no change needed. `shadow-cljs.edn` auto-discovers `*-test`; no allowlist.\n- ClojureDart writes `lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/semantic_register.dart` and `semantic_register/*.dart`; after the rename, stale `.dart` files for the deleted namespaces remain in `lib/cljd-out/` on every machine that built before (laptop, shiva, jupiter). The Dart peer build (`bb.edn:18-25`, `dart compile exe lib/cljd-out/yin/repl/slice-peer.dart`) imports by path, so a stale `semantic.dart` from the OLD kernel colliding with the NEW `semantic.dart` is the concrete Dart risk (§4).\n- Docs that must change: the 13 files above; `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` (836 lines, describes the v3 machine; 25 citers including `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `repl.cljc`, `ucf.cljc`, `docs/agents/format.md`); `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` (new revision record for `\"v4\"` as the one semantic contract); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:190, :390, :651, :3225, :3347` (phase-8 forward references); `yin.vm.linker.md:222-224` (§3 amendment); `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md` (SHA-256 prose, lowerer names); `semantic-register-vm.md` status header `:1-11` (\"working name… takes the name `yin.vm.semantic`\"); `derivations.md` §7.4-§8.3 PROPOSED to DECIDED/DONE; `continuation.md` §8.4; `docs/agents/build-n-test.md` if any lane names change (none expected).\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\nWARNING: load already refers to: #'clojure.core/load in namespace: yin.vm.encoder, being replaced by: #'yin.vm.encoder/load\n\nTesting yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test\nC4 preparing published py module\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_world.cljc:48:            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-support :as s]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
